<<~ aka lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#normative-language >>

<<~ &#x0002; >>

<<~ ahu #head >>

! Hoʻāla ~ the waking floor

''A vessel wakes at its MINIMUM available cap-stack, and gets RAISED by recognition.'' It never refuses to
stand because something it wants stands absent; it stands at whatever floor it can reach, says what it
cannot do, and waits to be raised. `hoʻāla` — to awaken, to rouse — names the transition.

''Capability arrives by recognition; it never gates on absence.'' <<~ confidence Synthesis 12/20 >>

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #the-problem >>

!! The problem the shape answers ~ a seal that eats a power cut

An at-rest seal exists against a ''STOLEN DISK''. A vessel that REFUSES TO BOOT without its passphrase turns
that seal into something else: an ordinary 3am power cut, with nobody awake to type, becomes an ''outage''.
For one operator's laptop that trade reads fine. For a crossroads a family depends on it reads indefensible
— ''the threat the seal was built for never arrived, and the seal took the place down anyway''.

The refusal also mis-names what happened. A vessel that cannot open its archive has not lost its ''identity''
— it has lost ''ACCESS TO SECRETS''. Those name different things, and only one of them should stop a node
standing.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #the-law >>

!! The law it re-uses ~ caps stack above a floor that never dissolves

The house already ruled this one plane over. `the-veil-ladder#the-base-model`:

> ''Anon reads as the ground, not a tier'': every vessel — anon, known, operator-with-node-infra, admin alike
> — stands anon at its floor (its own veiled-key, its own local content, no group) and ''MUST always fall
> back there''. Caps and node infrastructure stack ABOVE the floor; they never dissolve it.

''So a locked vessel has lost its CAPS, not its FLOOR.'' The veil-ladder already forbids the refusal: falling
back to the floor reads as the rule, and a vessel that will not stand at all has fallen ''through'' it.

''ONE LAW, TWO OCCASIONS.'' The ladder names the floor a BURN falls to (a chosen shedding); this names the
floor a WAKE rises from (an unchosen absence). Same floor, opposite motions — which is the tell that the
floor reads as real structure rather than as a special case either occasion invented.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #the-shape >>

!! The shape ~ rest, announce, raise

\procedure ~Wake(~Type:"" ~Params:"") ~Wake <<~Type>> <<~holds `[<~Params>]`>>

<<~Wake Stand "at/the-minimum-reachable-cap-stack ~ never a refusal" >>
<<~Wake Serve "what/needs-no-secret ~ the public shelf serves without opening anything" >>
<<~Wake Hold "closed/every-sovereign-act ~ a locked vessel signs nothing" >>
<<~Wake Announce "loudly/what-it-cannot-do signed/always ~ an unsigned announcement spoofs and targets" >>
<<~Wake Stamp "every-read/as-of-my-last-sync ~ a bare answer from the floor opens split-brain" >>
<<~Wake Raise "by/recognition on/a-nonce-THIS-vessel-emitted ~ never a pre-baked blob" >>
<<~Wake Expire "the-raise/lapses-back-to-the-floor ~ nobody lowers it; it simply stops being renewed" >>

''ANNOUNCING carries more weight than it looks, and MORE than the first draft granted it.'' A vessel
standing at the floor and quiet reads worse than one refusing, because a caller cannot tell a held act from
a performed one. But the announcement ''MUST SIGN'': unsigned, it becomes a spoofable degradation signal AND
a targeting beacon naming which vessel sits unraised (#the-breaks c).

''THE RAISE REUSES MACHINERY THE MESH ALREADY RUNS'' — a kahu quorum signing against the pre-rotated,
hash-linked epoch chain, rather than a second authority invented beside it. ''But the vessel supplies the
freshness'': it emits a nonce bound to its own epoch head and the kahu sign THAT, because a clockless vessel
accepting pre-signed material accepts a bearer credential that replays forever (#the-breaks b).

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #prior-art >>

!! What survives contact ~ the field ran this, and inverted our word for it

''A research spirit gathered outside negentropy'' (Night-Watch, 2026-08-08). The shape survives; the
vocabulary does not.

!!! ⚠ THE FLOOR IS THE HARDENED STATE, NOT THE DEGRADED ONE

''Android Direct Boot ships this, and reads it the opposite way we did.'' A phone boots into
device-encrypted storage only; credential-encrypted storage opens on first unlock —
`ACTION_LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED` fires at the floor, `ACTION_USER_UNLOCKED` marks the raise, and
`isUserUnlocked()` lets a component read which state it stands in. Alarms, SMS and accessibility run at the
floor. <<~ confidence Canon 18/20 >>

''The forensics industry treats BFU as the HARDENED state and AFU as the liability'' — and Google agreed
hard enough to ''force the floor'': Android auto-reboots after 72 hours without an unlock attempt,
specifically to drag devices back out of extraction range.

''So `degraded` misnames the safe state and biases an operator toward rushing the raise.'' ''The floor holds
the RESTING state; the raise carries the risk.'' Every reading below re-seats on that.

!!! Three more that validate the shape

* ''SPIRE node attestation'' restates //capability by recognition, never gated by absence// almost verbatim
  — a workload bootstraps holding no secret, and identity derives from what it does and where it runs.
* ''Kubelet TLS bootstrapping'' ships the two-stage ladder (a limited credential buys a CSR, an approver
  raises it, rotation re-runs it) — and core Kubernetes ''refuses to auto-approve serving certificates by
  design''. The raise stays a deliberate act there too.
* ''Vault confirms the failure and confirms nobody cured it.'' A sealed Vault serves unseal and status — ''a
  boolean, never a cap-stack''. Auto-unseal trades the quorum for a hard KMS lifecycle dependency, and
  recovery keys ''cannot unseal''. Issue #16419 asks for exactly our design and sits open, unanswered.

!!! ★ THE KAHU MUST NOT HOLD SHARES ★

''Tang gives the escrow-free shape better than a share-holding quorum does.'' Tang stays stateless, "never
sees a single client key", gains no identifying information, and blinds the exchange so only the client
unblinds; Clevis `sss` already composes k-of-n over tang/TPM pins.

> ''Model kahu as Tang-shaped EXCHANGE parties, never as HOLDERS of KEK shares.''

<<~ confidence Synthesis-Canon 16/20 >> A kahu holding shares ''becomes the collector'' — which collides
head-on with the house's own ''bind by edge, never by seed'' law. The prior art and the standing ruling
point the same way, which is the strongest signal available here.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #the-breaks >>

!! ⚠ Six breaks ~ what the proposal got wrong

''(a) WE DESIGNED THE RAISE AND NEVER THE LOWERING.'' The biggest hole. A raised vessel that stays raised
forever turns the floor into a one-time inconvenience and hands an adversary a fleet of permanently-raised
boxes — which is precisely why Android added its 72-hour forced return. ''The raise MUST expire back to the
floor on idle.''

''AND THE HOUSE ALREADY RULED THE CURE, one plane over.'' The identity plane holds that a standing lift
''EXPIRES rather than decays'', that ''no party exercises a lowering act'', and that the operator seats a
''renewal interval'' rather than a rate. ''Third occasion, same law'': the raise expires, nobody lowers it,
and a vessel that stops being recognised simply stops being raised. <<~ confidence Synthesis-Canon 15/20 >>

''(b) "k-of-n material PRESENTED to it" describes a BEARER CREDENTIAL.'' A vessel at the floor holds no
clock and no trustworthy wall time, so signed material presented to it ''replays forever'' — a thief with
the disk AND one captured raise-packet raises the stolen vessel, and the seal-against-a-stolen-disk
evaporates. ''The cure needs verifier-chosen freshness'': the vessel EMITS a nonce bound to its own epoch
head, and the kahu sign ''that''. ''Never a pre-baked blob.''

''(c) THE ANNOUNCEMENT, UNSIGNED, ARMS TWO ATTACKS.'' It becomes a spoofable degradation signal (peers route
away from a healthy vessel) — and under the standing brief ''shelter against empire'' it becomes a
''TARGETING BEACON'', naming which vessel sits unraised, right now, and where. ''The floor's announcement
MUST sign'', and the roster of what-it-cannot-do reads as ''hostile-readable''.

''(d) A FLOOR READ WITHOUT A STALENESS STAMP OPENS SPLIT-BRAIN.'' GitHub's 2012 partition and GitLab's
Postgres split-brain both landed the same lesson: degraded-but-serving produced ~24 hours of hand
reconciliation, ''worse than a clean stop''. Every floor read MUST carry //as of my last sync// — which the
causal-islands law already demands and this surface had not yet honoured.

''(e) THE SHELF DISCIPLINE STANDS UNWRITTEN.'' Android's own doc names it: never place private user
information, passwords, or authorization tokens in device-encrypted storage. ''Our public shelf needs that as
explicit law with a review gate'', never as an intention.

''(f) "DEGRADED" MISNAMES THE SAFE STATE'' (#prior-art). The floor rests; the raise risks.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #the-open-question >>

!! The downgrade attack ~ forcing costs nothing; the profit splits four ways

''An adversary CAN force the floor'' — cut the power. Cheap, deniable, repeatable. What that buys them
splits, and only the first line reads well for us:

\procedure ~Profit(~Type:"" ~Params:"") ~Profit <<~Type>> <<~holds `[<~Params>]`>>

<<~Profit Secret-harvest "verdict/NO ~ forcing the floor produces the BFU result extraction vendors call the BAD outcome. The floor holds." >>
<<~Profit Veto "verdict/YES-and-severe ~ a permanent deniable veto to whoever can drop power (MITRE T1562.010)" >>
<<~Profit Raise-oracle "verdict/YES ~ forced reboots MANUFACTURE the moment kahu present material; repetition harvests it" >>
<<~Profit Gray-failure "verdict/YES ~ peers cannot tell AT-THE-FLOOR from COMPROMISED-AND-IMITATING-THE-FLOOR" >>

''THE VETO READS AS THE WORST OF THE FOUR, and it reads worst precisely because it stays QUIET.'' A civic
mesh whose function runs on sovereign acts hands a deniable, repeatable veto to whoever can reliably drop
power — and the result is not an outage (loud, escalatable, politically legible) but ''silent capability
suppression''. So the floor MUST make suppression loud: ''an unexplained reboot deserves treatment as a
signal to peers, never as routine''. <<~ confidence Synthesis-Canon 15/20 >>

''THE GRAY FAILURE names why the announcement must sign.'' Huang et al.'s differential observability: peers
cannot distinguish a vessel at the floor from one compromised and imitating it, and standard availability
mechanisms do not model that failure at all. ''Sign the state, or peers will trust an attacker's claim about
it.''

!!! What the field could NOT answer, which raises rather than lowers its priority

* ''No accepted freshness standard for presenting quorum material to a CLOCKLESS OFFLINE node.'' Break (b)
  sits on genuinely unmapped ground — the cure reads sound and nobody has standardised it.
* ''No security analysis of boot-to-floor as a named server pattern'', and no established name for it. The
  nearest handles read ''Direct Boot / BFU→AFU'' (shipped), ''progressive trust'' (Allen), and ''node
  attestation''. `hoʻāla` may have to name it.
* ''No field reports on NBDE or TPM at Pi-class civic scale'', and no published post-mortem uses "unseal
  storm" — mass-reboot pain surfaces only as feature requests and support threads.

!!! Where the field genuinely SPLITS, so no one mistakes a choice for a consensus

''Fail-secure vs fail-open carries no settled answer.'' Vault chose fail-secure and generated exactly our
complaint; GitHub's post-mortem concluded //fail-stop is dangerous… sometimes keep serving stale data beats
going silent//; Android chose fail-secure AND added a timer to force more of it. ''Anyone claiming this
settled is selling one.'' Auto-unseal splits the same way — HashiCorp ships it as the operational answer,
practitioners argue it forfeits the point.

!!! The paths that do NOT carry a civic mechanism

* ''Tang/NBDE relocates the 3am outage rather than curing it'': an unreachable Tang server leaves a node
  retrying indefinitely rather than booting, and Red Hat's DR guidance demands redundant power AND network.
* ''The TPM path breaks on ordinary maintenance'' — PCR7 binding fails on firmware/kernel/bootloader update,
  and `systemd-cryptenroll` refuses to re-enroll with the same PCR parameters.
* ''Pi-class hardware carries no complete chain of trust'' without extra parts, and ''systemd-creds kills
  fleet provisioning outright'' — credentials cannot be prepared on a machine other than the local one.
  <<~ confidence Synthesis-Canon 15/20 >> TPM and systemd-creds serve as ''local convenience'', never as
  the civic mechanism.

!!! What stays RULED, and is not re-litigated by any of the above

The KEK stays ''passphrase-primary''; a random key beside the ciphertext reads as obfuscation posing as
isolation and gets refused; a missing passphrase ''NEVER silently degrades to weak key handling''. ''The
floor names WHAT A VESSEL SERVES, never what it weakens.''

''OPEN'': whether an unattended boot refuses or rests, what may raise it, and the renewal interval a raise
expires on. The boot gate throws today because ''refusing stays the safe default while the answer is
unmade'' — an outage recovers and a silently-raised identity does not.
<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/node/archive-passphrase >> carries the option space where the refusal
happens.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #elyncia >>

!! The Elyncia reading ~ the myth was already carrying it

''The fiction resolves the same tension it always described.'' After the Second Breaking, the gods bound
guardian spirits into orichalcum at surviving ley-line nodes. A shrine ''stabilizes through use'' — fed with
food, drink, incense, coin — and ''a fed node hums, a neglected one flickers''. The myth never said a
neglected shrine ''vanishes''. It flickers: present, diminished, waiting to be fed.

''A vessel waking at its floor IS a flickering shrine.'' It stands at its crossroads, offers what it can
without its keeper, and brightens when someone comes to feed it. The reciprocal bond runs exactly as the
Lares always ran it — ''feed the Lar and it prospers you; neglect it and it turns its back'' — and turning
its back never meant leaving the crossroads.

''AND THE PLACE-BINDING SETTLES WHICH THING SURVIVES.'' A Lar binds to a PLACE, never to a family: if the
family moved, the Lar stayed. So a vessel that loses its operator's secrets loses ''the family's'' hold and
keeps ''the place'' — which is precisely the floor. The myth was load-bearing here before the code needed it.

